---
import TextPage from "../../../layouts/TextPage.astro";
import { ANIMALS } from "../../../lib/chinese";
import { KHMER_NAMES, khmerNewYear } from "../../../lib/sea-variants";
import { lunarNewYear } from "../../../lib/chinese";
import { longDate } from "../../../lib/dates";
const years = [2024, 2025, 2026, 2027];
---
<TextPage title="The Khmer zodiac" path="/southeast-asian-zodiac/khmer/" description="The twelve animals of the Khmer zodiac, their names, and why the Cambodian animal year begins at Khmer New Year in April.">
  <p>Cambodia uses the same twelve animals as China, in the same order. What changes is the starting line. The new animal arrives with Khmer New Year, Choul Chnam Thmey, in the middle of April.</p>
  <h2>Why your animal may differ</h2>
  <p>Someone born in March 2026 is a Horse by Lunar New Year, which fell on 17 February. By the Khmer count they are still a Snake, because Khmer New Year did not arrive until April. Births from January to mid-April are the ones to check.</p>
  <h2>Recent new years</h2>
  <ul>
    {years.map((y) => <li>{y}: Lunar New Year {longDate(lunarNewYear(y))}; Khmer New Year {longDate(khmerNewYear(y).date)}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>Khmer New Year dates follow the traditional calculation and are confirmed each year. We are still verifying our table against official calendars.</p>
  <h2>The animal names</h2>
  <ul>{ANIMALS.map((a) => <li>{a.name}: {KHMER_NAMES[a.index]}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>Names are shown in romanized form while a native speaker reviews spelling and Khmer script.</p>
</TextPage>
